          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"When treated symbolically, butter or curds most commonly signify rich nourishment, material abundance, prosperity, and the provision associated with divine blessing.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Deuteronomy 32:13–14 — curds from cattle and milk from flocks appear among the rich provisions God gives Israel.\",\"Job 29:6 — Job's paths being washed with curds or butter poetically recalls his former prosperity.\",\"Job 20:17 — streams of honey and curds represent abundance that the wicked will not enjoy.\",\"Genesis 18:8 — curds and milk form part of Abraham's generous provision for his visitors, supporting associations with plenty and hospitality.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Hospitality, generosity, and honored treatment of a guest.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 18:8 — Abraham serves curds, milk, and meat to his visitors.\",\"Judges 5:25 — Jael gives Sisera milk or curds in a noble bowl, although the scene also carries irony because she subsequently kills him.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Basic subsistence amid agricultural devastation rather than uncomplicated prosperity.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 7:15, 21–22 — curds and honey are eaten after cultivated land is devastated; interpreters differ over whether this denotes remaining abundance, a simplified pastoral diet, or hardship.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Smooth or flattering speech that conceals hostility.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 55:21 — in translations using “butter,” the enemy's words are described as smoother than butter while war is in his heart.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The inevitable result of pressure, agitation, or provocation.\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 30:33 — pressing or churning milk produces curds or butter, as pressing anger produces strife.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A symbol specifically of spiritual nourishment, doctrinal richness, or mature revelation.\",\"basis\":[\"This appears in some allegorical or typological Christian interpretation, but it lacks a broadly recognized biblical convention and depends on extended analogy from milk.\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Butter or curdled milk has no single settled symbolic meaning across Christian interpretation. It is ordinarily a literal food whose contextual associations vary. Abundance and nourishing provision are the most recognizable symbolic associations, but passages such as Isaiah 7 prevent treating that meaning as universal.\"}"
        }
      ],
